#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial

#% text
# `finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(a, x)` gives the minimal polynomial of the finite field element `a` over the prime field, in the variable `x`, with integer coefficients from 0 to `p - 1`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(a, x, k)` gives the minimal polynomial over the subfield with `p^k` elements (`k` dividing the degree of the field); its coefficients are elements of the field of `a`.

#% item
# The minimal polynomial is the product of `x - c` over the distinct conjugates `c = a^(p^i)`; its degree divides the degree of the field.

#% item
# `minimal_polynomial(a, x)` gives the same for an element.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
F = finite_field(2, 4)

#% cell
finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(F([0, 1]), x)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An element of a subfield has a minimal polynomial of smaller degree:

#% cell
finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(F([0, 1])^5, x)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2

#% text
# Over the subfield with 4 elements:

#% cell
finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(F([0, 1]), x, 2)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(2, &(1 + &1 + &1^4), "Polynomial"), [0, 1, 1]) + x*finite_field_element(finite_field(2, &(1 + &1 + &1^4), "Polynomial"), [1]) + finite_field_element(finite_field(2, &(1 + &1 + &1^4), "Polynomial"), [1])*x^2

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The defining polynomial of a Conway field is the minimal polynomial of its generator:

#% cell
finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(F([0, 1]), x) == conway_polynomial(2, 4, x)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The minimal polynomial is the product of `x - c` over the conjugates `c = a^(5^i)`, here for an element of `F_(5^3)`; the difference expands to zero, and the element is a root:

#% cell
G = finite_field(5, 3)
b = G([1, 2])
m = finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(b, x)
[expand((x - b)*(x - b^5)*(x - b^25) - m), m /. x -> b]
#% output
#= [0, finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [])]

#% text
# Zero has the polynomial `x`, an element of the prime field a linear one:

#% cell
[finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(G(0), x), finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(G(3), x)]
#% output
#= [x, 2 + x]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The degree `k` of the subfield must divide the degree of the field (here 3); otherwise the call stays unevaluated:

#% cell
finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(G([1, 2]), x, 2)
#% output
#= finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1, 2]), x, 2)

#% text
# `k` equal to the degree of the field gives a linear polynomial `x - a`:

#% cell
finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial(G([1, 2]), x, 3)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [4, 3]) + x*finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `minimal_polynomial`, `finite_field_element_norm`, `finite_field_element_trace`, `conway_polynomial`
